course: dictionary
lesson: 3062
title: Economical: careful, good value, or not telling the whole truth
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Economical has three meanings, and the context tells you which one you need. In a maths shopping problem, «which pack is more economical?» asks which pack gives you the most for your money. «An economical car uses little petrol» means the car does not waste fuel.
explain: The third meaning is a joke. «Economical with the truth» means not telling the whole truth. It is informal and humorous, and it is mostly used in the UK. If the sentence is about lying or leaving out facts, economical is not about money.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Does «an economical car uses little petrol» still make sense if you say «a careful car uses little petrol»? Yes, so the first meaning fits. Try this every time you meet a new word.
fact: Economical means using money, fuel, time or materials carefully, without waste.
fact: Economical also means cheap to run or use, giving good value.
fact: «Economical with the truth» (informal, humorous, UK) means not telling the whole truth.
fact: «Telling porkies», «bending the truth» and «economical with the truth» all mean lying.
fact: «Pulling your leg» means fooling someone as a friendly joke. It is not the same as lying.
exercise: In a maths shopping problem, what does «which pack is more economical?» ask you to find? | Which pack is the heaviest to carry | Which pack is the biggest in size | *Which pack gives the most for the money | Which pack is the most colourful
exercise: «An economical car uses little petrol.» What does economical mean here? | *Careful with fuel, so none is wasted | Painted bright red | Very fast | Full of petrol
exercise: Which phrase is a friendly joke, not a lie? | Telling porkies | Bending the truth | *Pulling your leg | Economical with the truth
test: Mum buys the big pack because it is more economical. What does economical mean here? | Smaller and lighter to carry | Bought in the shop yesterday | Dearer, with a higher price | *Gives better value for the money
test: What does «economical to run» mean? | Hard to pick up and carry | Expensive to keep switched on | *Cheap to run, giving good value | Easy to run quickly across the park
exam: Which meaning of economical fits «She was economical with the truth about the broken vase»? | Painted in gold for a party | Careful with money, not wasting it | Cheap to run, giving good value | *Not telling the whole truth, an informal joke used in the UK
